[THOUGHT] The Analyst cannot specify available tests — so I must rely on standard, widely accessible point-of-care or basic lab/imaging tests. Given the patient’s severe COPD (FEV1 48%), key next steps include:  
- Assessing for polycythemia (a compensatory response to chronic hypoxemia), which can be detected via complete blood count (CBC);  
- Evaluating for cardiac strain or right heart involvement (e.g., cor pulmonale) using ECG — a simple, universally available test that may show right axis deviation, P pulmonale, or RBBB;  
- Chest X-ray to assess for hyperinflation, bullae, or alternative diagnoses (e.g., heart failure, interstitial changes).  
Among these, ECG is fastest, lowest-cost, and most likely to yield immediate actionable findings in severe COPD. It’s also highly sensitive for detecting right heart strain.

[TEST] 12-lead electrocardiogram (ECG)

